.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:57:**Sub-decision: where the categorisation rules live.** (i) Keep them as code and add more. (ii) Move the rule tables into packaged, validated data files like the policy YAML; the matching passes (reversals, round trips, pairing, profile names) stay in code. *Chose (ii)*: the lists of acquirers, lenders, cash wording and gambling operators are what changes most often, the v1 table can be kept as data for rollback, and a firm's own rules use the same format.
.bridgie/specs/statement-review/design.md:82:- **C1 Rule tables and categoriser** (src/cma/categorise.py; rule tables as data under src/cma/rules/). Built-in tables v1 (today's) and v2 (the new lists). v2 names cash paid in (counter, deposit machine, Post Office and PayPoint wording) as cash_deposit, and names cash-withdrawal wording that only the cash round-trip indicator reads. Precedence: per-line override, then protected built-in patterns (returned DD, director, capital, shareholder, lender, HMRC), then profile names (directors, own accounts), then firm rules, then the rest of the built-in table, then the default (a debit is supplier, a credit is unexplained). Each line records which rule set its category. A pattern that is ambiguous between buckets resolves to one that needs judgement, never to trading receipts; cash wording is not ambiguous, and counting cash as trading is the owner's decision (D31).
